Keep one task pointer, one wait queue, one atomic pending count, and one borrowed
rk_state pointer in execution/worker.c.
Use the supplied RK_WORKER_NAME when creating the task.

The proc callback checks actions_enabled before calling rk_worker_request(). The
request operation uses atomic_add_unless() to add one unless pending is already
INT_MAX. A false return means -EOVERFLOW with no wake; a true return means wake
the wait queue with wake_up_interruptible(). The thread uses
wait_event_interruptible() with pending > 0 || kthread_should_stop(), checks the
stop condition first, and records a run only when atomic_dec_if_positive()
returns a nonnegative value.

Start the worker before publishing procfs. On publication failure and unload,
kthread_stop() must wake the thread and wait for its exit before state is
destroyed.
